# NeoLeo intro sequence — working rules

## Scope
The intro is a self-contained overlay ("curtain") that renders ABOVE the
existing site. The existing site is finished and correct.

- Never modify existing page components, layout, styles or copy to
  accommodate the intro. If the intro seems to need a change there, stop
  and ask.
- All intro code lives under `components/intro/`. The only permitted change
  outside it is mounting the curtain and the head script described below.
- The site must render and function correctly with the curtain removed
  entirely. Verify this after every change.

## Process
- One step per request. Do not implement steps I haven't asked for, do not
  "also fix" adjacent things, do not add features you think are missing.
- Before writing code: state the plan — files added, files changed, and the
  approach — then wait for approval.
- After each step: state what to check in the browser to confirm it worked.

## Animation code
- All per-frame state lives in refs. Never call setState, never write to
  context, never trigger a React re-render inside an animation loop or
  useFrame. If you're about to, use a ref instead.
- One clock. Every phase reads elapsed time from the same source. No
  independent timers, no chained setTimeouts for animation timing.
- Every timing, offset, count, colour and easing goes in a single exported
  CONFIG object at the top of its file. No magic numbers inline.
- Beam origin and target are recomputed every frame. Never cache a rect.
- Keep the beam origin abstract — it is supplied to the FX layer, which
  must not know or care whether it came from a constant or a 3D projection.

## Three.js
- devicePixelRatio capped at 2. No shadows, no post-processing, no
  OrbitControls, no environment maps.
- Dispose geometries, materials, textures and the renderer on unmount.
- The GLB is an unrigged single mesh with arbitrary scale and origin.
  Always normalise via Box3 at runtime. Never hardcode a scale factor.

## Non-negotiables
- prefers-reduced-motion: skip the intro entirely.
- Every failure path (no WebGL, GLB missing, GLB slow, decode error) lands
  on the working site with no broken state and no blocked interaction.
- The curtain unmounts completely when finished. Never left at opacity 0.
- The real site content stays in the DOM throughout, for crawlers.
- Dev-only tooling (tuners, debug markers, replay) must not ship to
  production.

## Ask, don't assume
If a requirement is ambiguous, ask. Do not pick a direction and build it.
